<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamamoto Tsunetomo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1659–1719</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yamamoto Tsunetomo was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the Saga domain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> who served the Nabeshima lord Mitsushige. When his lord died in 1700 and retainers were forbidden to follow him in death, he became a Buddhist monk and retired to a hermitage. His conversations there with the younger samurai Tashiro Tsuramoto were recorded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagakure">Hagakure</a>, a classic of samurai ethics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamamoto-tsunetomo.jpg" alt="Yamamoto Tsunetomo" width="310" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamamoto_Tsunetomo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
